	abstract = {Visual-inertial odometry (VIO) plays a pivotal role in providing accurate 6-DOF pose estimation for robotics and autonomous systems. However, both classical geometric pipelines and recent learning-based approaches often struggle to preserve critical visual information and to represent complex motion dynamics, particularly under nonlinear or nonstationary trajectories. To address these challenges, we propose MixVIO, an adaptive visual-inertial odometry framework that integrates refined attention mechanisms with expressive kernel representations.A policy network is first employed to determine whether visual processing is necessary at each time step, enabling conditional execution of the visual branch. When activated, visual features are enhanced by a Refined Phase Attention (RPA) module, which injects spatial context and performs channel-wise recalibration to preserve salient structures. To model complex motion dynamics, a MixCore-KAN module augments LSTM hidden states using a hybrid basis of Gaussian and Lorentzian functions, improving flexibility in representing diverse motion patterns.Extensive evaluations on outdoor and indoor benchmarks demonstrate that MixVIO generalizes robustly under diverse sensing conditions. In particular, On the KITTI odometry benchmark, it achieves an average relative translation error of 1.69% while maintaining controlled visual usage, indicating that high-precision pose estimation can be achieved without uniformly invoking dense visual computation.  © 2026 IEEE.},
